## Key Research Findings

### The Loneliness Epidemic

- **WHO, June 2025:** Loneliness accounts for approximately **871,000 deaths per year globally** — about 100 deaths per hour
- **US Surgeon General, May 2023:** Social disconnection is **as dangerous as smoking 15 cigarettes a day**
- **APA "Stress in America," 2025:** 54% of US adults feel isolated often or some of the time
- **APA 2025 Survey:** 69% of US adults said they needed more emotional support than they received

### Blind and Disabled Employment

- **Only ~44% of working-age Americans who are blind are employed** versus 77% for people without disabilities (National Research and Training Center on Blindness and Low Vision)
- **Remote work driver:** Working from home drove a rise in employment of ~250,000 disabled workers by 2024, explaining 68-85% of post-COVID full-time employment gains for people with physical disabilities (Bloom, Dahl & Rooth, NBER 2024)

### Peer Support Effectiveness

- **BMC Medicine, 2024:** Consistent evidence that peer support improves hope, empowerment, and recovery for people with mental health challenges
- **Marmalade Trust, 2025:** 96% of participants felt "quite" or "extremely connected" after structured conversations about loneliness

### Populations We Serve

| Population | Key Stat | Source |
|------------|----------|--------|
| **Elderly isolation** | 33% of adults 50-80 felt lonely in past year | University of Michigan, 2024 |
| **Care home loneliness** | ~61% of residents moderately lonely, ~35% severely lonely | Gardiner et al. meta-analysis |
| **Refugee loneliness** | 15.9-47.7% experience loneliness (higher than general population) | Nguyen et al., 2024 |
| **Asylum seekers in limbo** | ~50% experience depression | Côté-Olijnyk, 2024 meta-analysis |
| **Reminiscence therapy efficacy** | Medium-to-large effect on reducing depressive symptoms in older adults | Frontiers in Psychiatry, 2023 |

